course: dictionary
lesson: 14281
title: Heading northward
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Northward means towards the north. On a map, north is usually at the top, so going northward means moving up the map. «The geese flew northward in spring.» The geese were moving towards the north.
explain: Northward can work in two ways. As an adverb it tells you where something moves: «The travellers moved northward, towards the cold.» As an adjective it describes a noun: «They set off on a northward journey.» Here northward tells you what kind of journey it was.
explain: In UK English you may also see northwards, which means the same thing as northward and is an adverb. Ask yourself: is the word showing movement, or describing a thing? That tells you which way it is working in the sentence.
fact: Northward means towards the north.
fact: «The geese flew northward in spring.» Here northward is an adverb, showing the direction they flew.
fact: «A northward journey» uses northward as an adjective, describing the journey.
fact: Northward and northwards mean the same thing in UK English.
fact: On most maps, north is at the top of the page, so northward means up the map.
exercise: «The geese flew northward in spring.» What does northward mean here? | *Towards the north | Very quickly | Towards the south | Over the sea
exercise: «They set off on a northward journey.» What kind of word is northward in this sentence? | A noun, naming a place | A verb, showing an action | *An adjective, describing the journey
exercise: «The travellers headed northward towards the cold.» Which way were they going? | In a circle | *Towards the north, where it is cold | Towards the sea | Towards the south, where it is warm
test: Fill the gap: «The birds flew ___ across the sea.» | *northward | eastward | southward | westward
test: Which phrase uses northward as an adjective? | the birds flew northward | they moved northward | the river ran northward | *a northward route
exam: Ella says northwards means the same as northward. Is she right? | No, they are opposite directions | *Yes, in UK English both mean towards the north | No, northwards means towards the south | No, northwards is a noun for a map
